No existing TableName methods in object — Casdoor relies on xorm default naming (struct name → snake_case, e.g. Role→role, Permission→permission). The default for `RelationTuple` would be `relation_tuple`. I declared a pointer-receiver TableName returning `rebac_tuple`. 

Concern: xorm's table name resolution via `names.GetTableName(mapper, v)` — it checks if the bean implements an interface. Let me recall xorm code: In `xorm.io/xorm` (casdoor uses github.com/xorm-io/xorm fork?), `names.GetTableName`:

```go
func GetTableName(mapper Mapper, v reflect.Value) string {
	if v.Type().Implements(tableNameType) { ... }
	if v.Kind() == reflect.Ptr {
		if v.Elem().Type().Implements(tableNameType) { ... call on elem ... }
	}
	...
}
```

where tableNameType is `TableName() string` (value receiver interface). A pointer-receiver method means `*RelationTuple` implements it but `RelationTuple` value does not. xorm usually checks `v.Type().Implements` for pointer and also dereferences. When Sync2(new(RelationTuple)), v is reflect.ValueOf(*RelationTuple)? They often do `reflect.ValueOf(bean).Elem()`. If Elem type RelationTuple doesn't implement the value interface (pointer receiver only), it might miss and fall back to mapper name `relation_tuple`, while queries using `&RelationTuple{}` might resolve via pointer... inconsistency risk.

To be safe, use a VALUE receiver: `func (t RelationTuple) TableName() string { return "rebac_tuple" }`. Then both T and *T satisfy interface. Let me change to value receiver.

Actually, do I even need a custom table name? Default `relation_tuple` is fine and avoids any issue. But "rebac_tuple" aligns with feature naming and my CRUD. Either works. Value receiver TableName is safest. Let me update.

Also fix DeleteRebacTuples per-bean.

